---
import Article from '../../layouts/Article.astro';
import Cite from '../../components/Cite.astro';
import Todo from '../../components/Todo.astro';
---

<Article
  title="Symmetry in programmable matter"
  kicker="Science · Distributed algorithms"
  status="Interactive coming"
  headline="Finding symmetry when every particle is blind"
  deck="Tiny robots on a hexagonal grid, each with constant memory and only its neighbours in view, work out together whether their shape is symmetric, in O(log⁵ n) time."
  facts={[
    { label: 'Where', value: 'Paderborn University, team project' },
    { label: 'When', value: 'Aug 2024 – Aug 2025' },
    { label: 'Model', value: 'Geometric amoebot model, reconfigurable circuits' },
    { label: 'Simulator', value: 'AmoebotSim (Unity)' },
    { label: 'Result', value: 'O(log⁵ n) symmetry detection' },
  ]}
  cites={['amoebots']}
>
  <figure class="figure">
    <svg viewBox="0 0 208 140" role="img" aria-label="A hexagon of 19 particles with a vertical axis of symmetry">
      <line x1="104" y1="2" x2="104" y2="136" stroke="#c8102e" stroke-width="0.8" stroke-dasharray="3 3"></line>
      <g fill="#111">
        <circle cx="76" cy="20" r="9"></circle><circle cx="104" cy="20" r="9"></circle><circle cx="132" cy="20" r="9"></circle>
        <circle cx="62" cy="44" r="9"></circle><circle cx="90" cy="44" r="9"></circle><circle cx="118" cy="44" r="9"></circle><circle cx="146" cy="44" r="9"></circle>
        <circle cx="48" cy="68" r="9"></circle><circle cx="76" cy="68" r="9"></circle><circle cx="132" cy="68" r="9"></circle><circle cx="160" cy="68" r="9"></circle>
        <circle cx="62" cy="93" r="9"></circle><circle cx="90" cy="93" r="9"></circle><circle cx="118" cy="93" r="9"></circle><circle cx="146" cy="93" r="9"></circle>
        <circle cx="76" cy="117" r="9"></circle><circle cx="104" cy="117" r="9"></circle><circle cx="132" cy="117" r="9"></circle>
      </g>
      <circle cx="104" cy="68" r="9" fill="#c8102e"></circle>
    </svg>
    <figcaption>Fig. 1: 19 particles, each seeing only its neighbours. The interactive version will let you reshape the cluster and watch the detection run.</figcaption>
  </figure>

  <h2>The model</h2>
  <p>
    In the amoebot model, particles sit on a hexagonal grid, have constant memory, and communicate only by
    “beeps” over reconfigurable circuits. Even simple global questions are hard when no particle can see the
    whole shape.<Cite id="amoebots" />
  </p>

  <h2>The approach</h2>
  <p>
    We reduced symmetry detection to polynomial identity testing over finite fields: encode the shape as
    binary strings, then check equality probabilistically with modular arithmetic, in parallel across the
    particle chain.
  </p>

  <h2>My part</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Distributed random number generation:</strong> a multi-round pipeline that generates and validates values 0 ≤ r &lt; p across all blocks.</li>
    <li><strong>SendValueToBlocks:</strong> a reusable two-round protocol that broadcasts a value from one block to all others over reconfigurable circuits.</li>
    <li><strong>ComputeF:</strong> polynomial evaluation f(r) = Σ(aₑrᵉ mod p) via right-to-left modular exponentiation, PASC-based tree summation, and a SubDivision2 subroutine for when the dividend is smaller than the divisor.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>What I learned</h2>
  <p>
    Thinking distributedly was hard at first. Every particle is blind to the global state, so you design
    local rules and trust them to produce the global answer. Once that clicked, the constraint became the
    interesting part.
  </p>
  <p><Todo>link the 21-page report, credit teammates and supervisors</Todo></p>
</Article>

